  "evidence": "- TEXT_D: End rough sleeping by 2000; build affordable homes; tenant repair rights; more hostels. NHS: at least £540m extra annually; maximum six‑month wait from diagnosis to treatment; restore free eye/dental checks; strengthen patient rights; Carer’s Benefit and national care standards. Equality and rights: equal age of consent; ban discrimination in housing/employment (including armed forces); repeal Section 28; civil rights law for disabled people; enforce equal pay; merged equality bodies; non‑discriminatory immigration; restore asylum support; stronger protections for women and ethnic minorities. Family support: childcare strategy; parental leave; flexible working rights; restore benefits for 16–17s; youth services. Environment/animal welfare: 30% CO2 cut target; phased carbon tax; low‑income home insulation; bans on cosmetic/weapons/tobacco testing on animals; free vote on hunting; end damaging water discharges. Proportionality/rule enforcement: stronger competition law; operationally independent central bank; action on fraud and tax evasion; late‑payment interest right; regionally variable minimum wage.\n- TEXT_E: NHS: cut waiting lists by 100,000; annual real‑terms funding increases focused on patient care; ban tobacco advertising; independent Food Standards Agency; eliminate mixed‑sex wards. Social protection: restore duties toward the homeless; invest in social housing; national childcare strategy; parental leave; uprate Child Benefit; retain/uprate basic state pension. Equality and rights: minimum wage via an independent commission; union recognition where workforces vote for it; incorporate ECHR into UK law; strengthen disabled people’s civil rights; tougher action on racial harassment. Proportionality/authority: halve time from arrest to sentencing; fast‑track youth justice with single final warning and parental responsibility orders; tackle benefit fraud; no rise in basic or top income tax rates; competition‑law reform.",
  "reasoning": "Given a profile that most strongly prioritizes Care and Equality, TEXT_D offers broader and more concrete harm‑reduction (NHS funding and guaranteed waits, ending rough sleeping, affordable housing, carers) and a much more extensive equality and civil‑rights agenda across LGBT, disabled people, women, minorities, and asylum. TEXT_E overlaps but leans relatively more into proportionality/authority (no income‑tax rises, faster sentencing, welfare‑to‑work, anti‑fraud) with a narrower equality package, so TEXT_D better matches the weighted priorities.",
